This research explores a Nirenberg-type problem on half spheres, given by the equation

$$\left\{
\begin
\begin{array}
\displaystyle -\Delta u +\frac{n-2}{n-2} \frac{|x|^{1-n}|u|^{n-1}}{|x|^{n}}=u^{p}  \quad \mbox{in } \mathbb{R}^n_{+},\\
\displaystyle u=0  \quad \mbox{on } \partial \mathbb{R}^n_{+}.
\end{array}
\right. $$

The investigation demonstrates the existence of a supersolutions and a weak solution with a minimal energy, applying the blow-up analysis of the solutions, and identifying the connection between this problem and its spherical counterpart. Notably, the research reveals that the solution properties diverge from their spherical counterparts, where the solution tends to concentrate at the boundary rather than in the domain. As a result, the concentration rate is determined by the distance from the boundary, and the solution behaves as $u(x)\sim (|x|^{1-n})^{\frac{1}{1-\mu(p)}}, $ with a larger exponent than in the spherical case, given by $ \frac{1}{1-(n-1)\mu(p)}$. The research also proves the nonexistence of the solution with a minimal energy for $p>1$ in the half-shere problem, and extends the existing nonexistence result from the full sphere problem to the case where $p>1$. The research further analyzes the solution property of the half-shere problem by supposing that the solution has a local bound with a Morse index i, and the solution tends to concentrate at the boundary with the exponent $\frac{i+1}{1-\mu(p)}$. Notably, the research improves the existing nonexistence result from the case of the full sphere problem to the case of the half-shere problem, and the solution tends to concentrate at the boundary with the exponent $\frac{i+1}{1-\mu(p)}$. The research further analyzes the solution property of the half-shere problem by supposing that the solution has a local bound with the index i and mass estimate $m_i\leq m_0$, and the solution tends to concentrate at the boundary with the exponent $\frac{i+1}{1-\mu(p)}$. The research extends the existing result from the case of the full
